# %% [markdown]
# ## 8. Feature Importance — Top Contributing Words
# Logistic Regression coefficients tell us which TF-IDF terms push a tweet
# toward "REAL DISASTER" (positive) vs. "NOT A DISASTER" (negative).

# %%
lr = models["Logistic Regression"]
feature_names = np.array(vectorizer.get_feature_names_out())
coefs = lr.coef_[0]

top_pos = np.argsort(coefs)[-15:][::-1]
top_neg = np.argsort(coefs)[:15]

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 7))
words = list(feature_names[top_pos][::-1]) + list(feature_names[top_neg][::-1])
weights = list(coefs[top_pos][::-1]) + list(coefs[top_neg][::-1])
colors = [DISASTER_COLOR if w > 0 else SAFE_COLOR for w in weights]
ax.barh(words, weights, color=colors)
ax.axvline(0, color="black", linewidth=0.8)
ax.set_title("Top TF-IDF Features Driving Each Class (Logistic Regression)", fontweight="bold")
ax.set_xlabel("Coefficient (→ Real Disaster | ← Not a Disaster)")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.savefig("../assets/feature_importance.png", dpi=140)
plt.show()

# %% [markdown]
# ## 9. Try it yourself

# %%
def predict_tweet(text, model=best_model, vec=vectorizer):
    clean = preprocess(text)
    vec_x = vec.transform([clean])
    proba = model.predict_proba(vec_x)[0, 1]
    label = "REAL DISASTER" if proba >= 0.5 else "NOT A DISASTER"
    return label, proba

for t in [
    "Massive flood in Ahmedabad — roads submerged near Sardar Bridge.",
    "My exam results are a complete disaster lol",
    "BREAKING: 6.2 magnitude earthquake strikes coastal region, tsunami alert issued",
    "this game's matchmaking is an absolute disaster today",
]:
    label, proba = predict_tweet(t)
    print(f"[{label:14s} {proba:.1%}]  {t}")

# %% [markdown]
# ## 10. Conclusion & Next Steps
#
# - **Logistic Regression** (TF-IDF, unigrams+bigrams) is the strongest,
#   most efficient model in this comparison — it edges out Linear SVM and
#   Naive Bayes on F1 and ROC-AUC while training in well under a second and
#   staying small enough (~140KB of weights) to run **client-side in a
#   browser**, which is what powers the "Live Classifier" tab of the
#   dashboard.
# - Random Forest and Gradient Boosting don't beat the linear models here —
#   expected for sparse, high-dimensional TF-IDF text features, where linear
#   decision boundaries tend to generalize better than trees.
# - **Stretch goal (not run in this notebook due to compute/time budget):**
#   fine-tuning a lightweight transformer (e.g. DistilBERT) typically pushes
#   F1 into the high 0.80s on this dataset by capturing context that
#   bag-of-words TF-IDF misses (e.g. sarcasm, multi-word idioms). A stub for
#   this is included in `src/bert_stretch_goal.py`.
#
# The trained Logistic Regression + TF-IDF vectorizer are exported to
# `models/production_model.json` for the interactive dashboard — see
# `src/train.py::export_production_model`.
